.TH QEMU\-TOOL 1 "September 2026" "qemu-tool 1.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
qemu\-tool \- run, generate and list QEMU virtual machines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qemu\-tool
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.I {quick\-start,check\-system,run\-vm,gen\-vm,gen\-compose,compose,ernic\-stats,lmcache\-stats,list,inspect,ssh,shutdown,man}
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B qemu\-tool
provides subcommands for managing QEMU-based virtual machines:
.TP
.B quick\-start
Boot two guests \(em each with an emulated GPU, NVMe and RDMA NIC \(em sized
for this host, bring Prometheus and Grafana up beside them, and run a GPU
test on each and an RDMA test across them. Nothing is built; the guest comes
from a published image. This is the fastest path to a working stack and the
one the daily
.I report\-for\-quickstart
lane runs.
.TP
.B check\-system
Ask every host question the stacks depend on and report the answers: the
tools on
.IR PATH ,
the Docker daemon and your access to it, KVM, the uid the containers will run
as, which settings file is in force, and whether the images directory has
room. Read-only, needs no
.BR sudo (8),
and writes nothing, so it is safe on a host you have not decided to use yet.
Exits non-zero if anything would stop a stack starting; warnings do not fail
it. The same checks
.B quick\-start
runs in its preflight, so the two cannot drift.
.TP
.B run\-vm
Launch an existing VM image created by
.BR gen\-vm .
.TP
.B gen\-vm
Create a new Ubuntu VM image using cloud-init seeding.
.TP
.B compose
Start the vfio-user GPU VM Docker Compose stack (rocm-ernic + rocjitsu),
wrapping
.B docker compose
with VM_NAME and VM_IMAGES_DIR pre-configured.
.TP
.B gen\-compose
Generate a scale-out compose stack \- an N-VM rocm-ernic mesh, one emulated
RDMA NIC and one rocjitsu GPU per VM \- from the fleet keys in a settings file.
.TP
.B ernic\-stats
Serve the stats files written by
.B "rocm\-ernic \-S"
as Prometheus metrics. Runs inside the compose stack; rarely invoked by hand.
.TP
.B list
List every QEMU VM running on this node, including VMs inside containers, and
identify which of them
.B qemu\-tool
started.
See
.BR "VM IDENTIFICATION" .
.PP
VM images are stored in
.I /var/lib/qemu-tool/images
by default.
The directory is owned by root and group\-writable by the
.B kvm
group.
Users must be a member of the
.B kvm
group to read or write images without
.BR sudo .
.SH SHARED OPTIONS
These options are accepted by both
.B run\-vm
and
.BR gen\-vm .
.TP
.BI \-\-env\-file " FILE"
Read settings from
.IR FILE
instead of searching the default locations.
.B Repeatable:
give it more than once and the files are layered left to right, a key set by
a later file overriding the same key in an earlier one and a key absent from
the later file keeping the earlier value. That is what lets one golden
settings file carry the ~20 keys every fleet shares while a small committed
overlay states only the handful that differ. See
.B SETTINGS FILE
below.
.TP
.BI \-\-vm\-name " NAME"
Name of the VM (default:
.IR qemu-minimal ).
Used to derive image file paths such as
.IR <images>/<NAME>.qcow2 .
May not contain a comma, which would terminate the QEMU option value it is
substituted into.
.TP
.BI \-\-arch " ARCH"
Target architecture:
.BR amd64 ,
.BR arm64 ,
or
.BR riscv64
(default:
.IR amd64 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-vcpus " N"
Number of virtual CPUs (default: 2).
.TP
.BI \-\-vmem " MiB"
Amount of guest RAM in MiB (default: 4096).
.TP
.BI \-\-images " DIR"
Directory where VM image files are stored (default:
.IR /var/lib/qemu-tool/images ).
.TP
.BI \-\-ssh\-port " PORT"
Host port forwarded to guest SSH (default: 2222).
.TP
.BI \-\-mac " ADDR"
Management NIC MAC address.
By default it is derived from
.BR \-\-ssh\-port
as
.IR 52:54:00:00:<hi>:<lo> .
Set it when the guest image pins its netplan to a particular MAC. Images
built by
.B gen\-vm
since
.I 3969f07
match on interface name instead and do not need this; images it built before
that commit, and images built elsewhere, may still pin one. Run
.I "grep \-A4 ethernets /etc/netplan/50\-cloud\-init.yaml"
in the guest to see what it pinned.
.TP
.BR \-\-kvm " | " \-\-no\-kvm
Enable or disable KVM hardware acceleration (default: enabled).
.TP
.BI \-\-qemu\-path " PATH"
Override the QEMU binary path.
By default the appropriate
.B qemu-system-*
binary is located via
.BR PATH .
.TP
.BI \-\-domain " FILE"
Load a libvirt domain XML file as the base configuration.
Use
.B \-
to read from stdin.
CLI flags override values from the XML.
.SH QUICK-START OPTIONS
.B quick\-start
takes one optional verb:
.BR all " (the default), " preflight ", " up ", " test ", " report " or " down .
.B preflight
checks this machine and touches nothing;
.B up
stops once the guests are booted;
.B test
configures and tests a stack that is already up;
.B down
stops it and leaves the overlays in place, so a later run skips the pull.
.IP
Anything still unknown is asked for on stdin. Every prompt has an answer it
takes on its own when stdin is not a terminal, so a CI run needs no flags to
stay non\-interactive \(em and the chosen answer is printed either way.
.TP
.BI \-\-env\-file " FILE"
Settings file. Defaults to the bundled
.IR qemu/env/quickstart ,
which ships inside the wheel, so this works with no checkout. Repeatable;
later files override keys set by earlier ones.
.TP
.BI \-\-stack\-dir " DIR"
Where the stack is generated. Default
.IR /var/lib/qemu\-tool/stacks/quickstart ,
falling back to a directory under
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME
when that is not writable \(em which on a fresh host it is not.
.TP
.BI \-\-key\-dir " DIR"
Where the guest keypair is pulled to. Default
.IR /var/lib/qemu\-tool/quickstart\-key .
The keypair ships as an ORAS referrer beside the guest qcow2, not inside it.
.TP
.BI \-\-images " DIR"
Directory for the guest qcow2 files, overriding
.BR VM_IMAGES_DIR .
Without it an unwritable default is asked about up front rather than failing
part way into the pull.
.TP
.BI \-\-report\-dir " DIR"
Write a markdown report here, and run the report step.
.TP
.BI \-\-repo\-root " DIR"
The checkout holding
.IR ansible/playbooks/fleet\-quickstart.yml .
Found automatically from a source install. An installed tool with no checkout
brings the guests up and skips the configuration and the tests, saying so.
.TP
.B \-\-size\-from\-host
Derive
.BR VM_VCPUS ", " VM_VMEM " and " VM_SHM_SIZE
from this machine, written to an
.I env.sized
layer beside the stack rather than into the settings file.
.BR "On by default" ;
.B \-\-no\-size\-from\-host
uses the values as written. The floors still apply: 4 vCPU, below which
.B ionic_rdma
never probes, and 4 GiB.
.TP
.B \-\-metrics
Bring Prometheus and Grafana up with the stack, with their data directories
beside it so neither needs root or a chown.
.BR "On by default" ;
.B \-\-no\-metrics
leaves both out.
.TP
.B \-\-in\-network
Reach the guests on the compose network rather than on published host ports.
For a caller that is itself a container attached to that network, which is
what the CI lane is.
.TP
.BR \-\-yes ", " \-y
Take the default answer to every prompt without asking.
.SH RUN-VM OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-filesystem " DIR"
Expose a host directory to the guest via virtio-9p.
.TP
.B \-\-vfio\-user\-root\-port
Place vfio\-user devices behind a PCIe root port.
.BR "On by default" ;
.B \-\-no\-vfio\-user\-root\-port
asks for the old topology, as does
.B VM_VFIO_USER_ROOT_PORT=false
in a settings file.
.IP
Without a root port the devices land on the root bus as conventional PCI
endpoints with no Express capability and a 32\-bit prefetchable BAR. For the
Ethernet path that costs only the reported link speed \(em
.I 0.000 Gb/s available PCIe bandwidth (Unknown x255 link)
from
.IR ionic .
For a GPU it is fatal. Measured on the emulated rocjitsu, same guest and
image, with only this changed:
.PP
.RS
.nf
off: Region 0: fd000000   (32\-bit, prefetchable) [size=16M]
on:  Region 0: c040000000 (64\-bit, prefetchable) [size=256M]
     Capabilities: [40] Express (v2) Endpoint
.fi
.RE
.PP
16M is far too small for a VRAM aperture, so
.IR amdgpu 's
memory controller cannot initialise and the probe unwinds with \-22. The
module still loads, so nothing looks wrong locally, no KFD node appears, and
the first complaint arrives from HIP as
.IR "no ROCm\-capable device is detected" .
.BR gen\-compose
warns when a stack renders GPUs with this turned off, because that message
names nothing about the cause.
.IP
It is necessary and not sufficient: 256M is still short of a gfx1250
aperture, and the remaining limit is the rocjitsu device model's BAR size.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-vfio\-user\-root\-port
Keep vfio\-user devices on the root bus as conventional endpoints. A GPU will
not bind
.IR amdgpu .
.TP
.BI \-\-vfio\-user\-switch\-width " N"
Group vfio\-user devices under PCIe switches,
.I N
per switch, in
.B \-\-vfio\-userdev
order. One
.B pcie\-root\-port
per switch, an
.B x3130\-upstream
on it, and an
.B xio3130\-downstream
per device. 0, the default, is off and leaves the flat tree.
.IP
Order is the pairing: nothing inspects what a socket serves, so the caller
decides what shares a switch by the order it lists the sockets. The
MI355X\-shaped stack interleaves its NIC and GPU sockets and asks for width
2, so each switch carries one of each and their traffic has a path that does
not cross the root complex.
.IP
Not representable in libvirt XML:
.B \-\-convert\-to\-libvirt
refuses a switched configuration rather than emitting a domain with a flat
tree.
.TP
.BI \-\-cpu\-sockets " N"
Pin the guest socket count. Without it QEMU infers
.IR sockets=cpus ", " cores=1 ,
so a guest given 8 vCPUs reports eight single\-core sockets. Must divide
.BR \-\-vcpus .
.TP
.BI \-\-pci\-hole64\-size " SIZE"
Size of the 64\-bit PCI hole as a QEMU size string, e.g.\&
.IR 512G .
The machine default does not fit many large BARs at once; eight emulated
GPUs will not enumerate without this.
.IP
Bounded by the CPU's physical address width, and the failure is a refusal at
startup rather than a degraded boot.
.B \-cpu EPYC
gives 40 bits, so on QEMU 10.2.1
.I 512G
is accepted and
.I 1T
is not; above that needs
.B host\-phys\-bits=on
or an explicit
.B phys\-bits=
on the
.B \-cpu
argument.
.TP
.B \-\-mgmt\-tap
Use tap+bridge for the management NIC instead of SLIRP.
.TP
.BI \-\-nvme " VALUE"
Attach NVMe storage.
A positive integer creates that many QCOW2-backed NVMe drives.
A negative integer creates
.B null_blk
devices.
Any other string is passed as literal NVMe device arguments.
.TP
.BI \-\-nvme\-trace " {doorbell,all,EVENT}"
Enable QEMU NVMe trace for the specified event category.
.TP
.BI \-\-nvme\-trace\-file " FILE"
Write NVMe trace output to
.I FILE
instead of stderr.
.TP
.BI \-\-nvme\-lbaf\-mask " HEX"
Restrict the advertised LBA format mask (hex bitmask).
.TP
.B \-\-nvme\-recreate
Recreate NVMe image files even if they already exist.
.TP
.B \-\-pci\-testdev
Attach the QEMU
.B pci-testdev
device.
.TP
.BI \-\-pci\-hostdev " BDF[,BDF]"
Pass through a host PCI device via VFIO.
Accepts a PCI BDF address (e.g.\&
.IR 0000:03:00.0 ).
May be repeated or comma-separated for multiple devices.
.TP
.BI \-\-vram\-dev\-index " N"
1-based index into the
.B \-\-pci\-hostdev
list identifying the GPU device for VRAM BAR mapping.
.TP
.BI \-\-vram\-bar " N"
BAR index for VRAM mapping (default: 0).
.TP
.BI \-\-vfio\-userdev " SOCK[,SOCK]"
Attach a vfio-user device via its Unix socket path.
May be repeated or comma-separated.
.TP
.B \-\-pci\-mmio\-bridge
Add a PCI-to-PCI bridge for extended MMIO window.
.TP
.BI \-\-data\-nic\-queues " N"
Number of virtio-net queues for the data NIC (default: 0, single-queue).
.TP
.BI \-\-mcast\-group " IP:PORT"
Attach a second NIC to the specified UDP multicast group.
.TP
.BI \-\-qmp\-socket " [PATH]"
Enable the QMP monitor socket.
Without an argument an automatic path under
.I /tmp
is used.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-qemu\-guest\-agent
Disable the QEMU guest agent channel.
.TP
.B \-\-backing\-shared
Mark the backing image as shared so multiple VMs can run from the same base.
.TP
.BI \-\-extra\-hostfwd " RULE"
Append an additional SLIRP host-forward rule, e.g.\&
.IR tcp::9150\-:9100 .
May be repeated.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the QEMU command that would be executed and exit without running it.
.TP
.BI \-\-convert\-to\-libvirt " [FILE]"
Emit a libvirt domain XML description instead of running the VM.
Write to
.I FILE
(default:
.IR <vm-name>.xml ),
or to stdout when
.B \-
is given.
.SH GEN-VM OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-mgmt\-tap
Use tap+bridge for the management NIC instead of SLIRP.
.TP
.BI \-\-size " GB"
Overlay image size in GB (default: 64).
.TP
.BI \-\-release " NAME"
Ubuntu release codename (e.g.\&
.IR resolute )
or version (e.g.\&
.IR 26.04 )
(default:
.IR resolute ).
.TP
.BI \-\-ssh\-key\-file " FILE"
Public SSH key to inject into the guest (default:
.IR ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub ).
.TP
.BI \-\-username " NAME"
Guest user account name (default:
.IR ubuntu ).
.TP
.BI \-\-user\-id " UID"
UID for the guest user account (default: 1000).
.TP
.BI \-\-password " PASS"
Guest user password (default:
.IR password ).
.TP
.BI \-\-packages " FILE"
Path to a cloud-init package manifest.
Pass
.B none
to skip package installation.
Default:
.IR /usr/share/qemu-tool/packages-default .
.TP
.B \-\-force
Overwrite an existing VM image.
.TP
.BR \-\-compact " | " \-\-no\-compact
Recompress the backing image with
.I "qemu\-img convert \-c"
once the bake finishes (default: enabled).
Compression is safe on a backing file because guest writes land in the
overlay and never rewrite a compressed cluster.
Skipped when
.B \-\-no\-backing
is set.
Use
.B \-\-no\-compact
for a fast local rebuild loop.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-backing
Create a single flat image rather than a backing/overlay pair.
.TP
.B \-\-restore\-image
Re-create the overlay from the backing image, discarding current VM state.
.TP
.BI \-\-backing\-file " FILE"
Use an existing image as the backing file instead of downloading a cloud image.
.TP
.BI \-\-ansible\-playbook " FILE"
Run Ansible against the VM after first boot using the specified playbook.
.TP
.BI \-\-ca\-cert " FILE"
Inject a CA certificate into the guest trust store.
.TP
.B \-\-ansible\-only
Skip cloud-init provisioning; only re-run Ansible against an existing backing image.
.TP
.BI \-\-backing\-image " OCI_REF"
Pull a published backing qcow2 from a container registry with
.BR oras (1),
decompress it, and create the overlay on top of it. No download from
cloud-images.ubuntu.com, no cloud-init and no bake: the same job as the
cloud-image path, from a different source. The artifact is not a runnable
image (artifactType application/vnd.batesste.vm-image.v1, one zstd layer), so
.B "docker pull"
will not fetch it. Requires
.B oras
and
.B zstd
on PATH. Mutually exclusive with
.B \-\-backing\-file
and
.BR \-\-no\-backing .
Re-running is a no-op unless
.B \-\-force
is given.
.SH GEN-COMPOSE OPTIONS
.PP
Generates
.I docker\-compose.yml
and
.I prometheus.yml
for an N-VM rocm-ernic mesh from the fleet keys in the settings file. VM 1 is
the mesh manager; VMs 2..N are workers that dial it.
.PP
Per-VM values are comma-lists, not numbered variables \-
.B VM1_NAME
does not survive eight VMs, let alone the mesh's sixty-four. A bare value
broadcasts to every VM; a list whose length equals
.B VM_COUNT
applies positionally; any other length is an error naming the key and both
lengths. The exception is a key naming a field that is already a list
.RB ( VM_PCI_HOSTDEV ,
.BR VM_VFIO_USERDEV ,
.BR VM_EXTRA_HOSTFWD ),
where the commas mean several values for
.I one
VM and the whole value goes to every VM. See
.I env/scale\-out
for the full key list.
.TP
.BI \-\-stack " NAME"
Stack to generate. Default:
.IR vfio\-user\-ernic\-rocjitsu\-scale\-out .
.TP
.BI \-\-env\-file " FILE"
Settings file. Same search path, and the same repeatable last-wins layering,
as every other subcommand. The generated header records one
.I "# env\-file:"
line per layer and a single
.I "# env\-sha256:"
over all of them concatenated, so an edit to the overlay invalidates the
render exactly as an edit to the golden file does.
.TP
.BI \-\-output\-dir " DIR"
Where to write. Defaults to the resolved stack directory, except that
generating into
.I /usr/share/qemu\-tool/compose
must be asked for explicitly: that tree is owned by the Debian package.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Exit non-zero if regenerating would change anything, and write nothing.
For CI.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the generated files to standard output instead of writing them.
.PP
The generated
.I docker\-compose.yml
carries a
.B "# env\-sha256:"
header recording the settings file it was built from.
.B compose
recomputes it and warns on standard error when the two disagree. It never
refuses to run:
.B down
against a stale tree is exactly how a bad edit is recovered from.
.PP
Four settings-file keys shape the generated fleet beyond its size. They live
in the settings file, not on the command line, because they have to match the
committed stack the next regeneration produces.
.TP
.B ERNIC_TAP
Give the guests working Ethernet. The rocm-ernic TCP mesh carries RDMA
payload only; without a TAP the guest driver transmits, the device drops the
frames, ARP goes unanswered, and
.BR ib_send_bw (1)
cannot complete the out-of-band exchange that precedes any inter-node
transfer. With this set, each ernic is attached to a TAP that a short-lived
privileged sidecar bridges onto a shared
.I l2
network, so every guest shares one L2 segment. The sidecar holds
.B CAP_NET_ADMIN
and exits; the long-running server needs only
.IR /dev/net/tun ,
because attaching to a pre-created, owned TAP requires no capability.
Default: false.
.TP
.B PROM_DATA_DIR
Host path bind-mounted as the Prometheus TSDB, relative to the stack
directory. A bind mount rather than a named volume so the record of a run is
visible to whoever made it, instead of being buried under
.I /var/lib/docker/volumes
as root and silently reused by the next fleet of a different size. Create it
and
.B "chown 65534:65534"
before
.BR up :
the container runs as
.IR nobody ,
and Docker creates a missing bind source as root. Default:
.IR ./prom\-data .
.TP
.B ERNIC_NOFILE
File-descriptor limit for every ernic service. Docker's default soft limit is
1024, which a mesh manager can reach inside a single run. Default: 65536.
.TP
.BR LOG_MAX_SIZE ", " LOG_MAX_FILE
Rotation for every service's log. Docker's json-file driver is unlimited by
default, and a wedged server with no backoff on its error path can fill a
disk. Defaults: 50m and 3.
.PP
Each ernic is additionally given its own
.B ERNIC_TCP_GUEST_GIDS
naming the GIDs its guest owns \(em the EUI-64 link-local derived from the
fleet MAC, and the guest IPv4. The mesh resolves a destination GID to a node
by asking each node what it owns, so this is inherently per-node and is
derived rather than configured.
.SH ERNIC-STATS OPTIONS
.PP
Parses the stats dumps written by
.B "rocm\-ernic \-S PATH"
and serves them on
.I /metrics
in Prometheus text format. Metric and label names match rocm-ernic's own
.IR prometheus/ernic\-exporter ,
so its Grafana dashboard works against this endpoint unmodified.
.TP
.BI \-\-stats\-dir " DIR"
Directory of
.I *.stats
files. Default:
.IR /run/ernic\-stats .
.TP
.BI \-\-port " PORT"
Listen port. Default: 9840.
.TP
.BI \-\-addr " ADDR"
Listen address. Default: 0.0.0.0.
.SH LMCACHE\-STATS OPTIONS
Serves LMCache coordinator metrics in Prometheus text format, the same shape
as
.BR ernic\-stats .
Where that one reads stats files written by each ernic, this one polls the
coordinator's HTTP API, so an unreachable coordinator is a scrape that
reports nothing rather than a crash.
.TP
.BI \-\-coordinator\-url " URL"
Base URL of the coordinator HTTP API. Default:
.IR http://127.0.0.1:9300 .
.TP
.BI \-\-port " PORT"
Listen port. Default: 9841, one past the ernic exporter so both can run on
one host.
.TP
.BI \-\-addr " ADDR"
Listen address. Default: 0.0.0.0.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " SECONDS"
Per\-request timeout when polling the coordinator. Default: 5.
.TP
.B \-\-once
Print one scrape to stdout and exit, instead of serving. Useful for checking
what a scrape would contain without standing up Prometheus.
.SH COMPOSE OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-vm2\-name " NAME"
Second VM's name, for the two\-VM stacks, passed through as VM2_NAME. Ignored
by a stack that has one guest, and by the generated scale\-out stack, which
bakes every VM name into its YAML.
.TP
.BI \-\-vm\-name " NAME"
VM name passed to the compose stack as VM_NAME (basename of the qcow2 without
extension).
Default:
.IR qemu-minimal .
.TP
.BI \-\-images " DIR"
Directory containing VM image files, passed as VM_IMAGES_DIR.
Default:
.IR /var/lib/qemu-tool/images .
.TP
.BI \-\-stack " NAME"
Committed stack to run. Default:
.IR vfio\-user\-ernic\-rocjitsu\-vm .
.TP
.BI \-\-stack\-dir " DIR"
Run a stack rendered by
.B "gen\-compose \-\-output\-dir"
.I DIR
instead of a committed one. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-stack ,
and required for a fleet rendered from an overlay:
.B VM_COUNT
and the VM names are baked into the generated YAML, so without it the
committed stack comes up instead, reporting success.
.IP
Defaults to
.BR $QEMU_TOOL_STACK ,
and failing that to the current directory when that directory is itself a
rendered stack, the way
.B "docker compose"
picks up a
.I compose.yaml
beside you. A rendered stack is recognised by the generator's provenance
header, not by the presence of a
.IR docker\-compose.yml ,
so an unrelated docker project is never adopted. There is no walk\-up: from
anywhere in a checkout it would reach the committed stack directory and drive
an 8\-VM stack under the name of a fleet.
.TP
.BI \-\-env\-file " FILE"
Settings file handed to
.BR "docker compose \-\-env\-file" .
Repeatable, and each layer is passed through as its own
.B \-\-env\-file
in the same order:
.B "docker compose"
applies them last-wins too, so compose and
.B qemu\-tool
resolve every key to the same value. See
.B SETTINGS FILE
below.
.IP
Against a rendered stack this is optional: the generated
.I docker\-compose.yml
records the layers it was built from and they are read back from it, so a
fleet needs no flags at all once it exists. The recorded names are resolved
as typed \(em an absolute name as itself, a relative one against the stack
directory and then the current directory, and nowhere else. A layer that is
at neither, or a generated stack naming no layers, is an error giving the
path and where it was looked for, never a fall\-back to the search path: that
is how a 32\-VM fleet ends up driven by an 8\-VM
.IR qemu/.env .
Passing
.B \-\-env\-file
overrides the memory, and layers that differ from the recorded ones are named
in the staleness warning, which still re\-hashes the files' current bytes.
.PP
.B compose
never regenerates and never edits a settings file: resizing a fleet is an
edit to
.B VM_COUNT
followed by
.BR "gen\-compose \-\-output\-dir" ,
so a re\-render is always a decision rather than a side effect of
.BR up .
Nor is it a command runner. For a whole fleet use
.B "ansible \-i"
.I <stack>/fleet\-inventory.yml
.BR fleet ,
which
.B gen\-compose
renders beside the compose file; for one guest,
.B inspect
prints a complete
.B ssh
command, and reports the ernic netdev and its address without a login.
.PP
Pass
.B \-v
to
.BR down .
Without it a killed run leaves its sockets in the shared
.I /run/vfu
volume and the next
.B up
dies on
.IR "cannot serve /run/vfu/rocjitsu\-1.sock: Address already in use" ,
seen as a dependency failing its health check.
.PP
All remaining arguments are forwarded to
.B "docker compose"
in the order typed, options included, so
.B "\-\-profile metrics up \-d"
needs no separator. A
.B \-\-
before the subcommand is still accepted and dropped rather than forwarded:
.B "docker compose"
reads one as end\-of\-options and silently ignored the profile that followed
it, bringing up the default services and reporting success.
.SH LIST OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit JSON instead of the table. The JSON form additionally includes the full
disk image path, full vfio-user socket paths, the owning user, the uptime in
seconds, and an
.B nvme
array per VM whose entries carry
.BR bdf " (canonical " dddd:bb:dd.f ", or null for an emulated device whose"
guest BDF is unknown),
.BR kind " (" emulated " or " passthrough ),
.BR source " (" argv " or " qmp ),
.BR qdev_id ,
and
.BR serial " or " class_verified
respectively.
.TP
.B \-\-qemu\-tool\-only
Omit VMs that were not started by
.BR qemu\-tool .
.TP
.B \-\-summary
Replace the per-VM table with a per-fleet aggregate and a measured load
picture. VMs are grouped by Docker Compose project; uncontainerised ones are
grouped as
.BR (uncontainerised) .
Four sections are printed: a header with the service roll-call,
.B HEADROOM
(how many more VMs fit by each ceiling, and which one binds),
.B LOAD
(CPU by service class, host load average, guest RAM resident, RDMA and
Ethernet totals, GPUs, logged-in users) and
.B OUTLIERS
(VMs far off the fleet's own CPU median, occupied guests, and any container
that is not running).
.IP
Every figure has an unavailable state and prints it rather than a zero: an
unreadable collector must not look like an idle fleet. The mesh and vCPU
ceilings are the measured walls recorded in
.BR AGENTS.md " issues 17 and 14."
.IP
Costs about 7 s against a 32-VM fleet, of which 5 s is the sampling window
\(em the ernic servers rewrite their stats at 1 Hz, so a shorter window
samples that rewrite rather than a rate. Plain
.B list
is unchanged and still never contacts a guest.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-guests
With
.BR \-\-summary ,
skip the only collector that contacts a guest: the logged-in-users line,
which costs about 2.8 s over 32 VMs and runs underneath the sample window.
The users line then reads
.B "not collected"
rather than zero, and the users row of
.B OUTLIERS
is omitted. An error without
.BR \-\-summary .
.PP
.B \-\-summary
and
.B \-\-json
compose: the JSON form is one object whatever the VM count, carrying
.BR sampled_seconds ,
a
.B source
per metric group
.RB ( proc ", " cgroup ", " ernic-stats ", " guest-agent ", " unavailable )
and units in the key names
.RB ( cores_busy ", " memory_mib ", " ip_bytes_tx ).
.PP
The table reports, per VM: PID, VM name, source, architecture, vCPU count,
memory, forwarded SSH port, whether KVM is enabled, uptime, the container name
when the VM runs inside one, any attached NVMe, and any attached vfio-user
sockets.
.PP
The
.B NVME
column lists every NVMe attached to the VM, emulated or passed through,
comma-joined, with identical labels collapsed as
.IR label *N
.RB ( VM_NVME=2
is ordinary, and two identical labels say no more than one plus a count), or
.B \-
when the VM has none. Four forms:
.RS
.TP
.B 00:05.0
The
.I guest
BDF of an emulated NVMe, read from the VM's QMP monitor. The
.B 0000:
domain is dropped for table width;
.B \-\-json
keeps the canonical form.
.TP
.B emul
An emulated NVMe whose guest BDF is not knowable \(em the VM has no QMP
socket, or the query failed.
.TP
.B host:03:00.0
A passed-through device, by its
.I host
BDF.
.TP
.B host:03:00.0?
The same, with no sysfs entry on this host to confirm it is an NVMe.
.RE
.PP
A host BDF and a guest BDF are
.I different address spaces,
which is why a passthrough entry is labelled rather than printed bare:
.B host:03:00.0
is the address on this machine, and the address
.BR lspci (8)
inside the guest reports is a different one. Only the guest BDF is actionable
inside the VM, and it is only available when the VM was started with
.BR \-\-qmp\-socket :
.B run\-vm
emits
.B \-device nvme,...
with no
.BR addr= ,
so QEMU picks the slot at realize time and the guest address never appears on
the command line.
.PP
Passthrough entries come from
.BR "\-device vfio\-pci,host=" ...,
which is equally how a GPU or a NIC is passed through, so the class is checked
in
.I /sys/bus/pci/devices/<bdf>/class
and only
.B 0x0108xx
(NVM Express) is claimed. A BDF this host has no sysfs entry for keeps a
trailing
.B ?
rather than being dropped, since dropping would silently hide a real NVMe.
.PP
The SSH column shows
.IR host \-> guest
whenever the two differ, which they do for a containerised VM: the guest's
.B \-hostfwd
port is the port inside that container's network namespace, and a fleet stack
gives every guest the same one. The host port \(em the one to actually
.BR ssh (1)
to \(em is the published mapping. In JSON output these are
.B ssh_port_host
and
.BR ssh_port .
An uncontainerised listing shows the single port, unchanged.
.TP
.B \-\-summary
Aggregate the listing per fleet and add a measured load picture: CPU, memory,
disk, RDMA mesh, GPUs and logins, with the headroom each one leaves. Takes a
5\ s sample, so it costs about 7\ s against a 32\-VM fleet; plain
.B list
is unchanged and still contacts no guest.
.IP
Three numbers are worth knowing before reading it. Memory is reported as the
cgroup's
.B shmem
figure, because guest RAM is a
.B memory\-backend\-memfd
and so is charged as shared memory, never to
.IR /dev/shm ;
the container's
.B anon
would understate a 32\-VM fleet twentyfold. The CPU line counts the ernic and
rocjitsu sidecars as well as QEMU, because on an emulated fleet the sidecars
burn more than the guests they serve. And GPU utilisation is reported as not
measurable rather than as a number, because the devices are emulated.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-guests
With
.BR \-\-summary ,
skip the one collector that contacts a guest \(em the logged\-in users line,
about 2.8\ s over 32 VMs. Everything else is host\-side. The users line then
reads
.I not collected
rather than zero, because nobody logged in and nobody asked are different
facts.
.SH INSPECT OPTIONS
Reports what a running VM actually has, joining the host\-side command line to
what the guest agent answers. Every field that cannot be established says so
rather than guessing: a device whose origin cannot be decided reads
.IR unknown ,
not
.IR emulated .
.TP
.I VM
Inspect a single VM, named positionally. Equivalent to
.BR \-\-vm\-name ,
which is kept because it is the spelling every other subcommand takes. Naming
the VM both ways with different values is an error rather than a precedence
rule nobody can remember.
.TP
.BI \-\-vm\-name " NAME"
Inspect a single VM. Default: every running VM.
.TP
.BI \-\-env\-file " FILE"
Settings file. Overrides the search path, and is repeatable with later files
winning key by key, as everywhere else.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit JSON instead of the human\-readable report. The JSON carries the same
fields, including
.BR ssh_keys " (fingerprint, comment, path and whether the guest accepts it),"
.BR block_devices ", " gpus " and " ssh_sessions .
.PP
Two fields repay explanation.
.B Key
names the key the guest will actually accept: it is read from that user's
.I authorized_keys
over the guest agent and matched against local candidates by fingerprint, so
.I accepted by guest
is a measurement rather than the configured default restated. A
.B cert\-authority
line is never offered, because a CA key signs certificates and does not log in.
.PP
.B Login
distinguishes
.IR none " from " unknown .
These guests have no
.IR /var/run/utmp ,
so
.B guest\-get\-users
answers with an empty list whether or not anyone is logged in; the answer
comes from systemd\-logind state files instead, read\-only, with no
.BR guest\-exec .
.I none
is returned only against positive evidence that a complete look happened \(em
an expired budget, a failed read or a filter that excluded an account all read
.I unknown
instead.
.SH CHECK-SYSTEM OPTIONS
Checks this host and prints a report grouped by area. Every line is marked
.BR ok ", " warn ", " FAIL " or " skip .
A
.B FAIL
is something that stops a stack starting and sets the exit status to 1; a
.B warn
limits what the host can do without blocking it \(em a missing
.IR /dev/net/tun ,
which only an
.B ERNIC_TAP
fleet needs, or a vCPU budget past what the mesh manager's untunable 20\~s
heartbeat tolerates. A
.B skip
is a check that did not apply.
.PP
The container identity is reported even when it is fine, because
.I which uid did my fleet run as
used to have a surprising answer: every stack ran as root until the
.B CONTAINER_USER
reference became unconditional. See
.B SETTINGS FILE
for that key.
.TP
.BI \-\-env\-file " FILE"
Settings file to check against, repeatable, later files winning key by key.
Without it the usual search path is used, and which file that found is itself
part of the report \(em a stale
.I qemu/.env
in a checkout outranks the system one, and a fleet then runs with
half-remembered values.
.SH MAN OPTIONS
Opens this page with
.BR man (1).
Only the Debian package installs it where
.BR man (1)
finds it unaided: the wheel ships it as package data, and neither
.BR uv " nor " pipx
has any mechanism for putting a man page on
.IR MANPATH ,
so an installed tool otherwise carries a manual nothing can read. The page is
handed to
.BR man (1)
as a file for exactly that reason.
.TP
.B \-\-path
Print the path to the bundled page and exit without opening it \(em for
piping into a pager of your own, or for adding to
.IR MANPATH .
.SH SSH OPTIONS
Opens an SSH session to a running VM, resolving the port, username and key
through exactly the same path
.B inspect
prints them \(em the username from the image's own sidecar rather than the
configured default, and an identity only when the guest names a key whose
private half is on this host. When it does not, no
.B \-i
is passed and a note says why before the password prompt appears; offering a
key the guest does not accept only buys a refusal.
.PP
Everything after the VM name goes to
.BR ssh (1),
so this tool's own options come
.I before
it \(em the order
.BR ssh (1)
and
.B docker exec
also take.
.TP
.I VM
Name of the running VM. Required.
.TP
.BI \-\-env\-file " FILE"
Settings file. Repeatable; later files win.
.TP
.B \-\-strict\-host\-key
Keep
.BR ssh (1)'s
own host\-key checking, which is off by default. Every guest built from one
backing image carries the
.I same
host key, and a fleet hands them consecutive ports on
.IR localhost ,
so port 2222 names a different machine on every fleet. Strict checking
therefore fails with
.I REMOTE HOST IDENTIFICATION HAS CHANGED
as a matter of course, and recording these in
.I known_hosts
would poison the entry for whatever uses that port next. The generated
.I fleet\-inventory.yml
passes the same pair, for the same reason.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the
.BR ssh (1)
command instead of running it.
.PP
To run a remote command, separate it with
.BR \-\- :
.PP
.EX
qemu\-tool ssh stebates\-fleet\-1 \-\- ibv_devinfo \-l
.EE
.SH SHUTDOWN OPTIONS
Asks each running guest to power off through the QEMU guest agent, then waits
for it to go.
.PP
This exists because stopping the container does not stop the guest.
.B run\-vm
execs QEMU, so PID 1 in a qemu container
.I is
.BR qemu\-system\-x86_64 ,
and QEMU exits on
.B SIGTERM
rather than ACPI\-shutting\-down the guest. A
.B docker compose down
is therefore a power cut: guest filesystems are left needing journal replay,
and on a 32\-guest fleet one came back with an aborted journal and a root
remounted read\-only. Raising
.B stop_grace_period
alone does not help, because nothing has asked the guest to stop \(em it only
makes Docker wait longer before the kill. Run this first, then
.BR down .
.PP
The guest agent is used rather than QMP because every guest already has one:
.B run\-vm
wires a virtserialport unconditionally, while QMP is opt\-in and off by
default.
.TP
.I VM
One VM to shut down. Default: every running VM.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " SECONDS"
How long to wait for guests to power off. Default: 120. Exceeding it warns and
names the guests still running rather than failing \(em the caller's next step
is to stop the containers regardless, and knowing which were not clean is more
use than a refusal.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
List what would be shut down and exit.
.SH VM IDENTIFICATION
.B qemu\-tool
stamps every VM it launches with two generic QEMU options:
.PP
.RS
.EX
\-name guest=<vm\-name>,debug\-threads=on
\-uuid <uuid5(namespace, "<role>:<vm\-name>")>
.EE
.RE
.PP
where
.I role
is
.B run\-vm
or
.BR gen\-vm .
.B list
recomputes the UUID from the parsed guest name and compares it against the
.B \-uuid
on the command line. An exact match identifies the VM as
.BR qemu\-tool 's
and reveals which subcommand started it, so a transient
.B gen\-vm
image-build VM is distinguishable from a running
.B run\-vm
VM.
.PP
Both options are generic rather than machine-specific, so they are accepted on
all supported architectures, including
.B riscv64
where
.B \-smbios
is not.
.PP
VMs shown as
.B external
were either started by something other than
.BR qemu\-tool ,
or were started by a version predating these markers; restart such a VM for it
to be identified. Identification is for operator convenience, not a security
boundary \(em the markers are plain command-line arguments and can be set by
anyone.
.SH SETTINGS FILE
Every
.B VM_*
key in the settings file maps to the option of the same name:
.B VM_VCPUS
is
.BR \-\-vcpus ,
.B VM_IMAGES_DIR
is
.BR \-\-images .
The file uses the docker compose env-file syntax, one
.I KEY=value
per line with no shell expansion, because
.B compose
hands the same file to
.BR "docker compose \-\-env\-file" .
An empty value is ignored.
.PP
Values are applied lowest to highest from the built-in defaults, a
.B \-\-domain
XML, the settings file, then explicit command-line options, so an option always
beats the file. One-shot actions
.RB ( \-\-dry\-run ,
.BR \-\-force ,
.BR \-\-restore\-image ,
.BR \-\-ansible\-only ,
.BR \-\-nvme\-recreate )
are deliberately not readable from the file.
.PP
The first of these that exists is used:
.BR \-\-env\-file ,
.BR $QEMU_TOOL_ENV ,
.IR ./.env ,
.IR qemu/.env ,
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/qemu-tool/env ,
.IR /etc/qemu-tool/env .
.PP
Only
.B \-\-env\-file
layers. The search path still resolves to exactly one file, so layering is
something asked for on the command line and never something inherited from
the ambient environment.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/qemu-tool/env
Site settings file, seeded from
.I /usr/share/qemu-tool/env/example
on first install and never overwritten afterwards.
.TP
.I /usr/share/qemu-tool/env/example
Settings template, documenting every key.
.TP
.I /var/lib/qemu-tool/images
Default directory for VM image files.
Owned by
.B root:kvm
with permissions
.B 2775
(setgid so new files inherit the
.B kvm
group).
Used only when it is writable; otherwise
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/qemu-tool/images
is used instead, so an unprivileged install still has somewhere to write.
.TP
.I /usr/share/qemu-tool/packages-default
Default package manifest installed into new VMs by
.BR gen\-vm .
.TP
.I /usr/share/qemu-tool/compose/
Compose stack directories used by
.BR compose .
.TP
.I /usr/share/qemu-tool/env/scale-out
The golden settings file for the scale-out fleet, documenting
.B VM_COUNT
and the rest of the fleet keys
.B gen\-compose
reads. It is the authoritative copy: a fleet that differs from it states only
the differing keys in an overlay named after it on the command line, rather
than forking the whole file. Not seeded into
.IR /etc ;
name it with
.BR \-\-env\-file .
The overlays themselves
.RI ( qemu/env/overlay\-ci ,
.IR qemu/env/overlay\-mi100 )
are not packaged: they name a particular CI lane and a particular host, so
they are only meaningful beside the repository that describes them.
.TP
.I /usr/share/qemu-tool/compose/vfio-user-ernic-rocjitsu-scale-out/
The generated fleet stack. Both files in it are written by
.BR gen\-compose ;
edit the settings file, not them.
.PP
The package manifests, the compose stacks and
.I env/example
are also bundled inside the Python distribution, so
.B pipx install qemu-tool
resolves them with no system install present. The
.I /usr/share/qemu-tool
copies take precedence whenever they exist.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a VM named
.I myvm
using the Noble release:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool gen\-vm \-\-vm\-name myvm \-\-release noble
.EE
.RE
.PP
Launch the VM:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool run\-vm \-\-vm\-name myvm
.EE
.RE
.PP
Dry-run with two NVMe drives and KVM disabled:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool run\-vm \-\-vm\-name myvm \-\-nvme 2 \-\-no\-kvm \-\-dry\-run
.EE
.RE
.PP
Emit a libvirt domain XML:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool run\-vm \-\-vm\-name myvm \-\-convert\-to\-libvirt myvm.xml
.EE
.RE
.PP
Start the vfio-user GPU VM stack:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool compose \-\-vm\-name myvm up
.EE
.RE
.PP
Bring up an eight-VM rocm-ernic fleet with metrics. Fetch the published
backing image once, overlay the rest on it, generate the stack, start it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool gen\-vm \-\-env\-file qemu/env/scale\-out \\
    \-\-vm\-name fleet\-1 \-\-backing\-image "$VM_BACKING_IMAGE"
for n in 2 3 4 5 6 7 8; do
    qemu\-tool gen\-vm \-\-env\-file qemu/env/scale\-out \\
        \-\-vm\-name fleet\-$n \-\-backing\-file images/fleet\-1\-backing.qcow2
done
qemu\-tool gen\-compose \-\-env\-file qemu/env/scale\-out
qemu\-tool compose \-\-env\-file qemu/env/scale\-out \\
    \-\-stack vfio\-user\-ernic\-rocjitsu\-scale\-out \-\-profile metrics up \-d
.EE
.RE
.PP
Confirm the committed stack is in step with the settings file, for CI:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool gen\-compose \-\-env\-file qemu/env/scale\-out \-\-check
.EE
.RE
.PP
Render and run the same fleet sized for a particular host, by layering that
host's overlay over the golden file. Only the keys the overlay states change;
everything else still comes from
.IR env/scale\-out :
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool gen\-compose \\
    \-\-env\-file qemu/env/scale\-out \-\-env\-file qemu/env/overlay\-mi100 \\
    \-\-output\-dir /var/lib/qemu\-tool/stacks/mi100\-fleet
qemu\-tool compose \-\-stack\-dir /var/lib/qemu\-tool/stacks/mi100\-fleet \\
    \-\-env\-file qemu/env/scale\-out \-\-env\-file qemu/env/overlay\-mi100 \\
    \-\-profile metrics up \-d
.EE
.RE
.PP
Both directory flags are required for an overlay fleet.
.B \-\-output\-dir
keeps the render off the committed stack, which
.B gen\-compose
writes by default and
.B \-\-check
compares against; and
.B \-\-stack\-dir
is what runs the render, since
.B VM_COUNT
and the VM names are baked into the generated YAML rather than read from the
settings layers at run time.
.PP
List every VM running on this node:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool list
.EE
.RE
.PP
List only VMs this tool started, as JSON:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool list \-\-qemu\-tool\-only \-\-json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Find the PID of a VM by name:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool list \-\-json | jq \-r '.[] | select(.name=="myvm") | .pid'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check stack status:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool compose \-\-vm\-name myvm ps
.EE
.RE
.PP
Tear down the stack:
.PP
.RS
.EX
qemu\-tool compose \-\-vm\-name myvm down
.EE
.RE
